    thinking: "**Revising Design Approach**\n\nI\'m now shifting gears to integrate the \"Bento Grid\" design theme. I\'m actively exploring how to reshape the app\'s editorial brutalist foundation with a bento-style structure. I\'m focusing on the modular, container-like nature of the bento approach to present content effectively within the terracotta color palette and light paper background.\n\n\n**Analyzing Bento Grid Details**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the Bento Grid\'s design language, as defined in the HTML and instructions. I\'ve noted the shift to a dark mode aesthetic, with a primary background of `#0A0A0A` and light text. I\'ve also identified key style elements like subtle borders and the elegant sans-serif font. The \"Bento Boxes\" will be secondary backgrounds that are `#111` or `#1A1A1A`. My focus is on extracting these styling rules and layout principles, while making no structural modifications to the underlying HTML.\n\n\n**Refining Color Palette Variables**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on color variables and how they\'re used. I\'m focusing on preserving the dynamic accent color from the Launch Center, mapping it to `--color-brand-accent`. I am now defining `--color-brand-bg` as `#0A0A0A` for the dark theme. Also, I\'m adjusting `--color-brand-black` to `#F5F5F5` for text. My next step is to examine `App.tsx` and related components to see these variables\' usage for a smooth dark theme transition.\n\n\n**Evaluating Component Adaptations**\n\nI\'m now diving into the component-level adjustments. I\'m focusing on key elements like `Navigation.tsx`, `Hero.tsx`, `About.tsx`, `Services.tsx`, `SkillsTools.tsx`, `CaseStudies.tsx`, `Testimonials.tsx`, `Contact.tsx`, and `Footer.tsx` to understand the specific styling changes needed to make them fit the polished dark Bento Grid theme. The aim is to convert the existing brutalist elements into premium-looking dark bento containers with appropriate border, background, and text color adjustments based on the Bento theme principles.\n\n\n**Revising CSS Variables**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the implications of redefining the global CSS variables. I\'m noting the strategic assignment of `--color-brand-black` to `#F5F5F5` for readability in dark mode. It seems that this color will now appear as off-white for the text, and it\'ll apply to elements like text and borders. The original thought was to set `border-brand-black` to `#000000` to create a perfect black border. Now, I\'m considering `border-brand-black` to a subtle white such as `/10` to keep the bento borders matching the style.\n\n\n**Implementing Dark Theme Styles**\n\nI\'m now integrating the bento box design from the provided template. My focus is on achieving a consistent dark theme across the application. I\'m noting the dark background (`#0A0A0A`), main text color (`#F5F5F5`), and the card backgrounds (`#111` or `#1A1A1A`). I\'m replacing the original brutalist elements with subtle borders and compact grid layouts, along with glowing hover animations. I\'m paying close attention to the CTA button styles (white with black text, or black with white text, or accent color).\n\n\n**Defining Bento Border Styles**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the aesthetic transformation of each Bento card. I\'ve decided to replace the brutalist shadows with refined borders, either white (`border-white/10`) or a dynamic accent (`border accent`). The goal is to provide each card with a dark, rich background color from the updated palette, transforming the app into a premium dark portfolio experience.\n\n\n"
